I'm going back to playground because this is

00:46.680 --> 00:48.040
a simpler file to read right now.

00:48.040 --> 00:50.640
Base counter has a lot of stuff going on.

00:50.640 --> 00:52.760
So the style block here,

00:52.760 --> 00:54.120
what exactly is going on here?

00:54.120 --> 00:56.080
Well, let's start by doing something pretty simple.

00:56.080 --> 00:59.400
Let's go ahead and just tag the entire page and just give it

00:59.400 --> 01:01.840
a background color of say,

01:01.840 --> 01:05.200
let's do the background color,

01:05.200 --> 01:10.760
Paya whip, and I can't spell.

01:10.760 --> 01:12.520
All right. That should do it.

01:12.520 --> 01:14.280
Okay. This is great.

01:14.280 --> 01:16.480
Now, what's going on here actually?

01:16.480 --> 01:20.520
What's going on is that when you just have a standard style tag,

01:20.520 --> 01:22.040
what view is doing is injecting

01:22.040 --> 01:24.920
the style basically across the entire app.

01:24.920 --> 01:27.400
So in other words, you see these buttons right here.

01:27.400 --> 01:31.880
If I go ahead and did button border red,

01:31.880 --> 01:34.920
or let's see 10px solid red like that,

01:34.920 --> 01:37.920
you'll see that it impacts everything.

01:37.920 --> 01:39.640
You might be thinking, oh,

01:39.640 --> 01:41.040
is it because it's app.view?

01:41.040 --> 01:44.680
It's no because the styles are being injected globally.

01:44.680 --> 01:47.000
So any component that comes on

01:47.000 --> 01:50.920
this page is going to get impacted by this.

01:50.920 --> 01:53.920
So this is a double-edged sword like anything else.

01:54.120 --> 01:58.160
There are times that you want CSS to cascade and do its thing.

01:58.160 --> 01:59.760
A lot of times actually though,

01:59.760 --> 02:00.920
you don't want it to do its thing.

02:00.920 --> 02:03.000
You want your styles to be encapsulated

02:03.000 --> 02:04.720
to the components you're using.

02:04.720 --> 02:06.040
So for instance,

02:06.040 --> 02:09.240
if we're looking at the user card in this case,

02:09.240 --> 02:14.880
you might want the buttons here to have a very specific style.

02:14.880 --> 02:16.920
In this case, let's say we wanted the buttons to be

02:16.920 --> 02:19.640
border 10px solid green.

02:19.640 --> 02:22.840
You'll notice that if we just use

02:22.840 --> 02:25.840
standard tile tag as it happens to be,

02:25.840 --> 02:27.600
it'll happen to override.

02:27.600 --> 02:32.120
While you might try to think of a way to reason why this is happening,

02:32.120 --> 02:34.840
trust me, when you have hundreds and thousands of components

02:34.840 --> 02:36.280
injecting global styles,

02:36.280 --> 02:38.240
you do not want to debug that.

02:38.240 --> 02:43.280
So this is like the first style of styling your app,

02:43.280 --> 02:44.720
which is you can just drop CSS,

02:44.720 --> 02:47.040
it will render everywhere.

02:47.040 --> 02:51.520
The one that's the most popular is something called scope styles.

02:51.720 --> 02:54.000
If you've never heard of scope styles before,

02:54.000 --> 02:56.000
there are basically a way to do exactly as

02:56.000 --> 03:00.440
the naming convention sounds to scope your styles to a specific area.

03:00.440 --> 03:02.400
So what is actually happening underneath the hood?

03:02.400 --> 03:03.920
Because I talked a lot about

03:03.920 --> 03:07.260
view building on what already exists in the ecosystem.

03:07.260 --> 03:10.960
Well, scope CSS, what it's actually doing if we take a look here,

03:10.960 --> 03:12.760
is it's actually taking a look at

03:12.760 --> 03:18.720
the elements that are relevant to this particular component,

03:19.360 --> 03:22.320
and it's appending a unique data attribute.

03:22.320 --> 03:25.880
So you see here this data.v.b600,

03:25.880 --> 03:30.240
that is what's basically being appended to the HTML.

03:30.240 --> 03:34.560
Then what's cool about this is if you look at the console,

03:34.560 --> 03:38.080
where's my CSS? Oh, it's down here, I hit it.

03:38.080 --> 03:39.320
Look at this style.

03:39.320 --> 03:43.160
It's button using the attribute selector to then go ahead and apply

03:43.160 --> 03:46.040
those styles specifically within that context.

03:46.040 --> 03:47.920
That actually works pretty well,

03:48.000 --> 03:49.720
assuming that the rest of your global CSS

03:49.720 --> 03:51.920
isn't written in a highly specific way.

03:51.920 --> 03:53.280
This actually works really great,

03:53.280 --> 03:56.280
and a lot of people have a lot of success using scope styles.

03:56.280 --> 04:01.080
But scope styles isn't perfect because if we went ahead,

04:01.080 --> 04:04.960
and I don't know, there's something a little bit more specific like this.

04:04.960 --> 04:08.820
I think that didn't quite do it. It's fine.

04:08.820 --> 04:11.520
I'll just do this. I'll just go the lazy route.

04:11.520 --> 04:14.800
If someone somewhere in your Enterprise app decided to bang

04:14.800 --> 04:19.280
port in the button, scope styles are not going to save you, by any means.

04:19.280 --> 04:21.880
You're still going to have to be stuck with the cascade effect.

04:21.880 --> 04:25.560
There's something to be said about being disciplined about writing your CSS,

